course: language
lesson: 4
title: Adjectives and adverbs: describing things and actions
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An adjective describes a noun (a scary dog, a red ball); an adverb tells you more about a verb: how, when, where or how often (she sang loudly, come soon).
explain: Piling up describing words doesn't make writing stronger: "the big, enormous, gigantic dog" says "big" three times; two sharp adjectives that each add something new are better.
explain: English speakers put adjectives in a set order without ever being taught it: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, where from, material, purpose; that is why "a big red ball" sounds right and "a red big ball" sounds weird.
explain: Many adverbs end in -ly, but not all (fast, soon, often), and some -ly words are adjectives (a friendly dog, a lonely cat); check what the word describes.
explain: Some describing words are old and irregular: good, better, best and bad, worse, worst; and places invent their own, like "bostin'" (brilliant) in the Black Country or "wicked" (very) in Boston.
fact: An adjective is a word that describes a noun; a synonym is a word with nearly the same meaning, like big and enormous.
fact: Adjectives usually go in this order: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose, as in "a lovely little old round red French wooden toy box".
fact: An adverb tells you more about a verb, an adjective or another adverb, answering how, when, where or how often.
fact: Many adverbs end in -ly, but fast, soon and often are adverbs too.
fact: Friendly, lovely, silly and lonely end in -ly but are adjectives, because they describe nouns.
fact: In standard English good is an adjective (a good game) and well is the adverb (you played well); "played good" is common in casual speech, especially in America.
fact: Well can also be an adjective meaning healthy, as in "I feel well".
fact: The comparative compares two (bigger, more beautiful); the superlative names the top one (biggest, most beautiful); long adjectives use more and most.
fact: Good, better, best and bad, worse, worst are irregular comparisons.
fact: "Most bestest" doubles the top form; it is fine for fun, but school writing says "the best".
fact: Wicked means very in New England in the US (wicked good), brilliant in British slang, and evil in its old standard meaning.
fact: In UK slang mint means excellent; in London bare means very or lots of; in the Black Country and Birmingham bostin' means brilliant.
fact: Literally first meant exactly, really happening; for hundreds of years writers, Charles Dickens among them, have also used it just to make things stronger, and some dictionaries list both meanings.
exercise: What does an adjective do? | Describes a noun | Describes a verb | Joins two sentences | Names a person
exercise: Ruby writes "The big, scary, hairy, smelly, enormous, gigantic dog". Why does her teacher say "choose two"? | Some words repeat the same idea, which weakens the writing | Sentences may only have two adjectives by a school rule | Dogs cannot be scary and hairy at once | Long sentences are always wrong
exercise: Which pair are synonyms? | enormous and gigantic | scary and smelly | hairy and big | big and small
exercise: Which sounds right to most English speakers? | a big red ball | a red big ball | a ball big red | a red ball big
exercise: In the usual adjective order, which comes first? | Opinion, like lovely | Colour, like red | Material, like wooden | Purpose, like toy
exercise: Which word is the adverb in "She sang loudly"? | loudly | sang | she | sang loudly
exercise: Which of these is an adverb even without -ly? | soon | sooner-ly | slow-ish | quickness
exercise: Sienna says "friendly" is an adverb because it ends in -ly. What does "a friendly dog" show? | It describes a noun, so it is an adjective | It describes a verb, so it is an adverb ending in -ly | It is a noun, the name of a dog | It is a verb, because dogs act friendly
exercise: Jayden's coach says "You played good today!" What would school writing expect? | You played well today | You played goodly today | You played better-ly today | You played best today
exercise: Zoe asks for the right pattern. Which is correct? | good, better, best | good, gooder, goodest | good, more good, most good | good, better, bestest
exercise: How do we compare a long adjective like beautiful? | more beautiful, most beautiful | beautifuller, beautifullest | beautifuler, beautifulest | most beautifuller, more beautifullest
exercise: Ali writes "the most bestest day ever". What should school work say? | the best day ever | the most best day ever | the bestest day ever | the most goodest day ever
exercise: Callie's US cousin from Boston says the pizza is "wicked good". What does she mean? | Very good | Evil but tasty | Burnt and bad | Good for witches
exercise: Lucas says the film was "proper mint, bare good". What does he mean? | It was excellent, really good | It tasted of mint | It was boring and empty | It was cheap to watch
exercise: Mila's teacher says "I'm literally dying of boredom". What is she doing? | Using literally to make it stronger, not for real | Telling the class she is truly dying, right there and then | Making a grammar mistake no one has made before | Using a word invented by teenagers last year
exercise: In "The fast car drove fast", what is the second "fast"? | An adverb, telling how it drove | An adjective, describing the car | A noun, the name of the car | A verb, the action of the car
exercise: How many adjectives are in "The lonely old man walked slowly to the friendly little shop"? | Four: lonely, old, friendly, little | Three: lonely, slowly, friendly | Five: lonely, old, slowly, friendly, little | Two: old and little
exercise: Put "wooden, ugly, small, Italian" in the usual order before "chair". | an ugly small Italian wooden chair | a small ugly wooden Italian chair | an Italian ugly small wooden chair | a wooden small ugly Italian chair
test: Your mate writes "the massive huge giant spider". What would you tell him? | Pick one size word and add a different detail | Add three more size words to make it scarier | Swap them into alphabetical order | It's perfect, more adjectives are always better
test: Your little cousin says "a wooden old lovely box". How would most people say it? | a lovely old wooden box | an old wooden lovely box | a wooden lovely old box | a box lovely old wooden
test: Gran from Dudley says "Ow bist? That cake was bostin'!" ("Ow bist" means how are you.) What does she think of the cake? | It was brilliant | It was burnt | It was too sweet | It was boring
test: A teacher asks "Which word tells you how often in 'We often swim'?" | often | swim | we | none, often is a noun
test: Your brother insists "every -ly word is an adverb, end of." What sentence proves him wrong? | That was a lovely day | She ran quickly | He spoke softly | They left early
test: A boomer coach shouts "you guys did rad today, played real good!" ("Rad" means great.) In school writing, how would you put his praise? | You played very well today | You played rad good today | You played goodly today | You played real goodest today
test: What would happen if English had no adverbs? | You couldn't easily say how, when or where things happened | Nothing, nouns would simply do the same job as adverbs | All verbs would disappear too | Sentences would have to be questions
test: Zoe says "worse" must be a spelling mistake for "badder". What's true? | Bad, worse, worst is an old irregular pattern | She's right, it should be bad, badder, baddest | Worse is the American spelling | Worse and badder are both school English
test: Grandad, hearing "wicked" from a Bostonian, thinks the pizza is evil. What's the best way to explain? | In Boston wicked means very, so it's very good | It really is an evil pizza, with something nasty baked in | Wicked is a Scottish word for cold | Wicked means small in America
test: A Gen Z kid says "she literally ate the exam, no cap." What's going on? | Literally is used to make it stronger; she did really well | She really ate the exam paper | She failed the exam badly | She copied the exam from someone
test: Your mate says "Dickens never used literally just to exaggerate, kids started that." What's true? | Writers like Dickens used it that way long ago | He's right, it began last year | It began on TikTok | Nobody has ever used it to exaggerate
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of an adverb? | It qualifies a verb, an adjective or another adverb, indicating manner, time, place or frequency | It designates a person, place or object | It joins two clauses of equal rank | It replaces a noun to avoid repetition
exam: Select the sequence of adjectives consistent with the established conventions of English. | a lovely old wooden box | a wooden old lovely box | an old lovely wooden box | a wooden lovely old box
exam: Identify the correctly formed comparative and superlative of the adjective "good". | better, best | gooder, goodest | more good, most good | better, bestest
exam: In respect of the word "friendly" in the phrase "a friendly dog", which statement is consistent with the established facts? | It is an adjective, notwithstanding its termination in -ly | It is an adverb, by reason of its termination in -ly | It is a noun denoting the dog | It is a verb describing the action of the dog
